  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Widespread showers and thunderstorms this afternoon and\n   evening, with gusty outflow winds of 30-50 mph, brief moderate rainfall,\n   and lightning, strongest storms south of Highway 200.\n\n-  Another round of storms Wednesday capable of 30-50 mph outflows\n   and heavy rain rates up to 0.5 inches per hour.\n\n-  Persistent active pattern late week into the weekend under\n   southwesterly flow, with a 15% chance of a tropical moisture\n   tap on Sunday or Monday.\n\n\nToday & Tonight: High pressure ridging transitions to \nsouthwesterly flow today, driving afternoon temperatures roughly \n10 degrees above normal. Conditions will deteriorate this \nafternoon as an atmospheric disturbance lifts northward out of \nLemhi and southern Idaho counties into western Montana, triggering\nwidespread showers and thunderstorms. The strongest convective \nactivity is anticipated south of Highway 200 in western Montana, \nwhere storms will be capable of producing gusty outflow winds of \n30 to 50 mph, frequent lightning, and brief moderate rainfall. \nActivity will taper off and exit the forecast area by midnight.\n\nWednesday through Weekend: A stronger disturbance will traverse \nthe Northern Rockies on Wednesday morning-mid afternoon, renewing\nthe threat for gusty outflow winds of 30 to 50 mph. Heavy \nrainfall potential will be enhanced with this second wave, where \nstronger storm cores may produce rainfall rates up to 0.50 inches \nper hour. Additionally, extreme southwest Montana will contend \nwith gusty winds and low afternoon relative humidity Wednesday \nafternoon and evening and again Thursday, potentially creating \ncritical fire weather conditions.\n\nSouthwesterly flow aloft will persist Thursday into Friday as a \nbroad upper-level trough settles over the Pacific Northwest. This \npattern will keep temperatures near to slightly below late-summer \naverages alongside periodic showers and thunderstorms, \nparticularly during peak afternoon and evening heating. Guidance \nremains in solid agreement that troughing will linger over the \nregion through the weekend, with roughly 15% of ensemble members \nindicating a potential tap of significant tropical moisture Sunday\nor Monday. Regardless of whether this tropical plume \nmaterializes, the Northern Rockies will remain embedded in an \nactive, unsettled weather pattern through the weekend.",
